# Everything the build does not need, and several things it must NEVER receive.
#
# SECURITY. `.env` and `private/` are the load-bearing entries. A Docker build
# context is uploaded to the daemon wholesale, and anything copied into a layer
# is readable by anyone who can pull the image — deleting it in a later layer
# does not remove it from the earlier one. `.gitignore` protects the repository;
# this file is the equivalent protection for the image, and the two are separate
# mechanisms that have to be maintained separately.
#
# The Dockerfile copies named paths rather than `COPY . .`, so these are defence
# in depth rather than the only guard. Keep them anyway: the next person to add
# a `COPY` line will not re-derive this reasoning.

# Live credentials: Supabase host, user and password.
.env
.env.*

# The engineering audit, including a worked exploit chain.
private/

# Version control and CI.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Editor and agent tooling.
.vscode
.claude
.elixir_ls
graphify-out

# Build artefacts from the host. These are architecture- and path-specific;
# letting a Windows or x86 _build/ leak into an arm64 image is a subtle way to
# produce a broken release.
_build
deps
cover
doc
.fetch
*.ez
*.beam
erl_crash.dump

# Node modules, if assets ever grow a package.json.
assets/node_modules

# The digested output of a previous local `mix assets.deploy`. The image runs it
# itself; a stale manifest copied in would silently win.
priv/static/assets
priv/static/cache_manifest.json

# Test support and docs — not needed to build a prod release.
test
docs
*.md
!README.md

# The image builds its own; never inherit one.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
